    <h2>Humidity Makes the Shoals a Washing Market</h2>

    <p>Sitting in a humid river valley, the Shoals gives mold, algae, and mildew everything they need — moisture, shade, and warmth much of the year. It shows up as green streaks on north-facing siding, black spotting on concrete, and the slick film that builds on a shaded walkway. The growth is relentless, which is what makes exterior cleaning a genuine recurring need here rather than a once-a-decade chore. Every town in the region, from the newer Muscle Shoals subdivisions to the historic Florence and Sheffield streets, generates the same steady demand to get the green off.</p>

    <h2>Not All Grime Comes Off the Same Way</h2>

    <p>The mistake that damages houses is treating every surface like a driveway. Flat concrete can take real pressure, and blasting it is fine. Point that same wand at old brick, aging mortar, wood or older siding, or a roof, and the pressure carves the surface, strips out mortar that's a century old, and forces water behind the cladding where it does hidden damage. The Shoals is full of exactly those vulnerable surfaces. The professional distinction is between pressure washing and soft washing — soft wash uses low pressure and a cleaning solution to do the work chemically instead of mechanically, so the mildew dies and rinses without the surface paying for it.</p>

    <h2>Why Historic Brick Needs a Softer Hand</h2>

    <p>On the masonry throughout the Florence and Sheffield historic districts, and the older brick commercial buildings downtown, soft washing isn't a preference — it's the only safe method. A practitioner reads the surface first: what it's made of, how old the mortar is, how sound it is, and whether water could get behind it. Then they choose the lowest pressure and the right solution that will clean it. The honest admission the "pressure washing" name obscures is that most exterior cleaning worth doing on an older Shoals home is actually soft washing, and anyone who only owns a high-pressure setting shouldn't be pointing it at your hundred-year-old brick. Killing the growth at the root is also why a proper soft wash stays clean for a year or more while a plain rinse is green again by summer.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Pressure Washing Costs in the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Concrete and driveways</strong> — roughly $0.15 to $0.40 per square foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Whole-house soft wash</strong> — commonly $250 to $600 by size and number of stories.</li>
      <li><strong>Driveway or patio</strong> — often $150 to $350.</li>
      <li><strong>Recurring commercial accounts</strong> — quoted per visit, the backbone of a steady route.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Delicate historic surfaces cost a little more because soft washing them properly is slower and more careful than opening the pressure up on a slab — and it's the difference between a clean wall and a damaged one.</p>

    <h2>The Recurring-Account Opportunity</h2>

    <p>The smartest money in this trade is repeat work, not one-off jobs. Because the mildew always comes back in this climate, houses and especially commercial storefronts along Court Street and the older downtown cores need cleaning on a schedule — twice a year for many surfaces. A washer who books recurring residential and commercial accounts instead of chasing one-time cleanings builds a predictable route and a reliable income, and the customer gets a building that stays presentable year-round instead of sliding back to green between rare cleanings.</p>

